In **Node.js (CommonJS)** there are **3 main ways** to export a module.

# 1️⃣ Export Single Thing (Default Export)
-----------------------------------------------
You export only one function/object/value.

### math.js

function calculateTax(amount) {
  return amount * 0.18;
}

module.exports = calculateTax;


### app.js


const calculateTax = require("./math");

console.log(calculateTax(1000));


### Real-time Use Case

👉 Payment service exporting only one main function
Example: **payment gateway calculation**

---

# 2️⃣ Export Multiple Things (Named Exports using object)
-----------------------------------------------
You export many functions together.

### orderService.js

function createOrder() {
  console.log("Order created");
}

function cancelOrder() {
  console.log("Order cancelled");
}

module.exports = {
  createOrder,
  cancelOrder
};


### app.js

const orderService = require("./orderService");

orderService.createOrder();
orderService.cancelOrder();

### Real-time Use Case

👉 E-commerce order module
Create / Cancel / Update / Track order in same file

---

# 3️⃣ Export Properties One-by-One
-----------------------------------------------
Attach values directly to `exports`

### auth.js

exports.login = function() {
  console.log("User logged in");
};

exports.logout = function() {
  console.log("User logged out");
};

### app.js

const auth = require("./auth");

auth.login();
auth.logout();

### Real-time Use Case

👉 Authentication module
login, logout, verifyToken functions

---

# ⚠️ Important Interview Point

`exports` is just a shortcut of `module.exports`

But ❌ this will break:

exports = function() {}   // WRONG

---

# Quick Revision Table

| Method                | When Used         | Example Project     |
| --------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| `module.exports = fn` | One main feature  | Payment calculation |
| `module.exports = {}` | Group features    | Order service       |
| `exports.xyz = fn`    | Utility functions | Auth module         |

